From: lavachemist <48248311+lavachemist@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] hwmon: pwm-fan: cut the fan supply in system sleep

pwm_fan_suspend() turns the PWM off, but in the default enable mode
(pwm_disable_reg_enable) it keeps the fan's regulator enabled, so the fan
stays powered for the whole system sleep. On the KONKR Pocket FIT that is
the fan_pwr/fan_vdd 5 V pair (GPIO 125/124), and switching it off for the
sleep window took deep sleep from about 47 mAh/h to about 10-18 mAh/h
(~0.27 W). Disable the supply after the PWM in suspend, whatever the enable
mode. Resume already restores it: set_pwm() powers the fan on again (supply
first) as soon as the saved PWM value is non-zero.
---
--- a/drivers/hwmon/pwm-fan.c
+++ b/drivers/hwmon/pwm-fan.c
@@ -721,8 +721,17 @@
 static int pwm_fan_suspend(struct device *dev)
 {
 	struct pwm_fan_ctx *ctx = dev_get_drvdata(dev);
+	int ret;
 
-	return pwm_fan_power_off(ctx, true);
+	ret = pwm_fan_power_off(ctx, true);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+
+	/*
+	 * The default enable mode keeps the supply on with the PWM off, which
+	 * would leave the fan powered for the whole system sleep.
+	 */
+	return pwm_fan_switch_power(ctx, false);
 }
 
 static int pwm_fan_resume(struct device *dev)
